{"title":"Intersection (set theory)","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The <b>intersection</b> of two or more <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Set\" title=\"Set\">sets</a> is the set of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Element\" title=\"Element\">elements</a> that are common to all of them.  Thus, the intersection of the sets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/5/62511f4d99c1621775385afa593225a56e41f220.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1, 2, 3\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/b/d1b8b6ed60dbfb6af2c9c405ba9967b1ae04940f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1, 3, 5\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"18\" /> is the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/d/9cd3573b267baf925eafe3e048db6a1be46142b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1, 3\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" />.  \n</p><p>Intersection is denoted by the symbol <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/f/c0f2b8e02812dd6278501da3698b07563864df3d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cap$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"11\" height=\"11\" />, so the preceding example could be written <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/986f76c66439d443c62778e96dd6039bbfda09db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1, 2, 3\\} \\cap \\{1, 3, 5\\} = \\{1, 3\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"211\" height=\"18\" />.  One can also use the symbol for intersection in the way one uses a capital sigma (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/f/c8f77e3035db5fe9a4975967750ac1a6454bda8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Sigma$\" width=\"12\" height=\"12\" />) for sums, i.e. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/3/d73f3ebc0d9459482bb4be9816381cd9ab4c7541.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\bigcap_{i = 1}^n A_i = A_1 \\cap A_2 \\cap \\ldots \\cap A_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"213\" height=\"48\" /> is the intersection of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> sets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/c/5ec3ef7916f1c34ae5947f297d1cbb10be1b4a5a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"112\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Properties\">Properties</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Intersection_(set_theory)&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Properties\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li>For any sets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/f/bef747a48a2a8d43c4841e12c89f2de2502a672e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"35\" height=\"16\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/a/d1a1614cb289d2d4760bd01704e0f089353cada8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A \\cap B \\subseteq A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"86\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/5/d25f437b82bf26e0e54731968e6b523cba96048d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A \\cap B \\subseteq B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"87\" height=\"15\" />.  Thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/6/99666118c30b97dbeaeff9529e48fb2483ba6344.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A \\cap B = A$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"86\" height=\"13\" /> if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/5/3254647aba215297c432d4a99a5a72b6f45fcbdb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A \\subseteq B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"52\" height=\"15\" />.</li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Intersection_(set_theory)&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Subset\" title=\"Subset\">Subset</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Union\" title=\"Union\">Union</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Intersection_(set_theory)&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018091657\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.016 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.021 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 84/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 316/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 2581/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    6.175      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    6.175      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:30393:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018091657 and revision id 239936. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}